[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The failure is on the leaf’s uplink interfaces toward the spines. On leaf_router_0_1, both uplinks are administratively down while the host-facing port remains up. Evidence: ip -br link on leaf_router_0_1 shows eth0 DOWN and eth1 DOWN, while eth2 (10.0.1.1/24) is UP. Consequently, BGP on the leaf cannot form adjacencies to the spines; show ip bgp summary on leaf_router_0_1 shows both neighbors 172.16.0.6 (spine_router_0_0) and 172.16.0.10 (spine_router_0_1) stuck in Active. The routing table on the leaf has only its directly connected LAN (10.0.1.0/24), with no learned fabric routes. This explains the symptom: pc_0_1 can ping its default gateway on the same LAN (10.0.1.1 via eth2), but anything off-subnet (including pc_0_0 at 10.0.0.2) is unreachable because the leaf is isolated from the fabric.

Localization: leaf_router_0_1 interfaces eth0 (172.16.0.7/31 to spine_router_0_0) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31 to spine_router_0_1). Subsystem: Linux interface state/L2, impacting FRR BGP peering.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_1 uplink interfaces eth0 and eth1 were administratively shut (DOWN), severing both spine adjacencies.

Corrective fix: bring up the uplinks and let BGP reconverge. On leaf_router_0_1, run:
- ip link set dev eth0 up
- ip link set dev eth1 up
Verify: show ip bgp summary becomes Established to 172.16.0.6 and 172.16.0.10, and ip route shows fabric prefixes. Optionally, ensure these interfaces are auto-enabled at boot in the node’s network configuration to prevent recurrence. === END ===